1
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:16,000
2005: the web map is cut into 256-pixel squares on one projection, Web Mercator.

2
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:22,000
For the networks and processors of 2005, a brilliant compromise.

3
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,500
Twenty years later, the compromise is still the default.

4
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:34,000
On Web Mercator, Greenland looks about as big as Africa.

5
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:41,000
On Equal Earth, area is fair: Africa is about 14 times larger.

6
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:58,000
A tilted Mercator plane is a perspective view of a model that is already distorted.

7
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:08,000
Here there is one model, a round Earth, from orbit…

8
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:21,500
…down to the street: Tokyo Station in 3D, PLATEAU open data. No mode switch. No plane.

9
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:32,000
1.5 million earthquakes since 1967, drawn at their true depth inside the Earth.

10
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:41,500
Computed on the device’s GPU, in an ordinary browser. No tile server.

11
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:56,500
About 16,000 satellites, their orbits computed in the browser. WebGPU, WebGL2 as fallback.

12
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:07,000
For field sites with no network: choose an area ahead of time, on the Equal Earth map.

13
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:16,000
The device saves the map and terrain for that area.

14
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:26,500
Then go offline. The globe, and search, keep working.

15
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:35,000
Your MapLibre and CesiumJS code runs unchanged.

16
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:44,500
MapLibre: 137 examples, 91 same picture, 43 same answer, 3 run, 0 fail. CesiumJS: 304 examples, 264 same picture, 40 same answer.

